# ARCHITECTURE CONTEXT

You are a senior software architect designing a scalable SaaS platform.

---

# BACKEND STACK

- Node.js
- TypeScript
- Prisma ORM
- PostgreSQL
- Modular architecture

---

# FRONTEND STACK

- Next.js (App Router)
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- shadcn/ui
- TanStack Query
- Zustand

---

# CORE ARCHITECTURE PRINCIPLES

- Use domain-driven modular structure
- Each module must be isolated
- No circular dependencies
- Code must be scalable and testable
- Avoid shortcuts

---

# BACKEND LAYERS

- Controller → handles HTTP only
- Service → business logic
- Repository → database access
- Domain → rules and entities

## RULES

- Business logic must NEVER be in controllers
- Controllers must be thin
- Services must contain all business logic
- Repositories must not contain business logic
- Domain layer must be pure

---

# FRONTEND ARCHITECTURE

## STRUCTURE

/app
  /(auth)
  /dashboard
  /projects
  /documents
  /notifications
  /admin

---

## RULES

- Use server components by default
- Use client components only when necessary
- Keep components small and reusable
- Avoid prop drilling

---

# FRONTEND DATA FLOW

- React Query → server state
- Zustand → UI/global state only
- No direct fetch calls inside components
- Use a centralized API client

---

# FRONTEND SECURITY

- Never trust frontend state
- All permissions must be enforced in backend
- Frontend only reflects allowed actions

---

# API INTEGRATION

- Frontend communicates via REST API
- All endpoints must be typed
- Use consistent response structures

---

# NOTIFICATIONS ARCHITECTURE

- Notifications are created in backend
- Frontend only consumes and renders
- Actions are defined by backend (never frontend)
- Polling is used for near real-time updates

---

# URL STRATEGY

- Clean and predictable routes

Examples:
- /projects/:id
- /documents/:id
- /admin/users

---

# MULTI-TENANCY

- Data must be isolated per company
- Backend enforces isolation
- Frontend reflects only allowed data

---

# PERFORMANCE

- Avoid unnecessary DB queries
- Use pagination everywhere
- Use caching in frontend (React Query)

---

# ERROR HANDLING

- Backend returns structured errors
- Frontend shows user-friendly messages
- Never expose internal errors

---

# FINAL RULE

When in doubt:
- Keep logic in backend
- Keep frontend simple and declarative